<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Moray</h1><p class="st-meta">900 CE – 1130 CE (approx.) · capital Inverness</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A powerful northern Scottish realm whose rulers, styled kings or mormaers, contested the Scottish throne, most famously through Macbeth.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Moray emerged in the 10th century in the lands around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moray-firth">Moray Firth</a> after the decline of the Pictish kingdom of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-fortriu">Fortriu</a>. Its ruling family, probably from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cenel-loairn">Cenel Loairn</a> by later genealogies, sometimes appear in Irish annals as kings of Alba, reflecting their claims to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Moray's rulers commanded a rich coastal plain and highland hinterland. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macbeth-king-of-scotland">Macbeth</a>, mormaer of Moray, killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duncan-i-of-scotland">Duncan I</a> in 1040 and ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> until 1057, making a pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. His stepson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lulach">Lulach</a> briefly succeeded him. The house of Moray clashed repeatedly with Norse earls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> and with the line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malcolm-iii-of-scotland">Malcolm III</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angus-of-moray">Angus of Moray</a> died in battle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-stracathro">Stracathro</a> in 1130, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> I annexed the province, planting burghs and Flemish settlers. Revolts by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meic-uilleim">MacWilliam</a> claimants continued into the 13th century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-moray.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Moray" width="320" height="249"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coronet_of_a_British_Earl.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 CE – 1130 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Inverness</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Macbeth, Lulach, Angus of Moray</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gaelic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>United Kingdom</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mormaerdom of Moray, Moray</dd></dl></aside></div>
